README

This library implements the ZeroMQ publish-subscribe pattern (PUB and SUB
sockets) for Circle. It speaks the ZeroMQ wire protocol ZMTP 3.0 over TCP (see
https://rfc.zeromq.org/spec/23/), so it can talk to libzmq and its language
bindings (e.g. pyzmq, cppzmq, JeroMQ, NetMQ) and to other Circle systems
using this library.

libzmq itself cannot be used with Circle, because it depends on POSIX
threads and BSD sockets, so the protocol has been implemented from scratch
on top of the Circle TCP/IP stack (CSocket).


FEATURES

* CZMQPublisher (PUB socket): sends messages to all subscribers, which
  subscribed a matching topic. Peers can be ZeroMQ SUB or XSUB sockets
  (e.g. the XSUB side of a zmq_proxy()).

* CZMQSubscriber (SUB socket): subscribes topics and receives messages
  from publishers. Peers can be ZeroMQ PUB or XPUB sockets (e.g. the XPUB
  side of a zmq_proxy()).

* Both sockets can Bind() to a TCP port (the peers connect) and/or
  Connect() to one or more remote endpoints, in any combination, like with
  libzmq. Outgoing connections are established in the background and
  re-established automatically, when they get lost.

* Multipart messages with up to ZMQ_MAX_FRAMES (16) frames and frames with
  up to ZMQ_MAX_FRAME_SIZE (256 KByte) each.

* Topics are byte prefixes of the first frame of a message, like with
  libzmq. The publisher filters the messages before they are sent. The
  subscriber filters again (needed for XPUB peers). Subscriptions are
  reference counted: a topic, which has been subscribed twice, has to be
  unsubscribed twice.

* Only the NULL security mechanism is supported (no PLAIN or CURVE
  authentication/encryption). Use it in a trusted network only.

* ZMTP 3.1 PING commands (ZMQ_HEARTBEAT_* options of the peer) are answered
  and ZMTP 3.1 SUBSCRIBE/CANCEL commands are understood, but the library
  announces itself as ZMTP 3.0, so libzmq peers use the 3.0 subscription
  format.

Not supported: other socket types (REQ/REP, PUSH/PULL, DEALER/ROUTER),
other transports (ipc://, inproc://, udp://, pgm://), ZMTP 1.0/2.0 peers
(libzmq older than 4.0) and the high-water mark options.


USAGE

	#include <zeromq/zmqpublisher.h>
	#include <zeromq/zmqsubscriber.h>

	// publisher, subscribers connect to "tcp://<ip-of-raspberry-pi>:5556"
	CZMQPublisher *pPublisher = new CZMQPublisher (&m_Net);
	pPublisher->Bind (5556);

	pPublisher->Publish ("sensor.temp", "21.5");		// [topic, string]
	pPublisher->Publish ("sensor.raw", pData, nLength);	// [topic, binary]

	CZMQMessage Message;					// any multipart message
	Message.AddFrame ("sensor.all");
	Message.AddFrame (pData, nLength);
	pPublisher->Publish (Message);

	// subscriber, connects to a publisher on "tcp://192.168.0.10:5557"
	static void MessageHandler (const CZMQMessage &rMessage, void *pParam)
	{
		// rMessage.GetFrameCount (), GetFrame (i), GetFrameSize (i), ...
	}

	CZMQSubscriber *pSubscriber = new CZMQSubscriber (&m_Net);
	pSubscriber->RegisterMessageHandler (MessageHandler, this);
	pSubscriber->Subscribe ("pc.");			// "" subscribes all messages
	pSubscriber->Connect ("192.168.0.10", 5557);

Instead of registering a message handler, you can derive a class from
CZMQSubscriber and override its method OnMessage().

The sockets must be created with new (they must not be deleted) after the
network subsystem has been initialized. A publisher drops messages, while
no subscriber is connected, exactly like libzmq, so a subscriber misses the
first messages after it has connected ("slow joiner").

To build the library, do (after building the Circle libraries):

	cd addon/zeromq
	make

Link your application with addon/zeromq/libzeromq.a and the network
libraries (see sample/Makefile).


IMPLEMENTATION

Each ZeroMQ socket is a CTask (the "I/O task"), which polls all connections
to its peers (non-blocking) about every millisecond, drives the ZMTP
handshake, answers PING commands, processes subscriptions and calls the
message handler of the subscriber. Each Bind() and Connect() creates an
additional helper task, which accepts incoming connections or connects to
the remote endpoint in the background, because these operations block in
the Circle network stack.

Publish() sends directly from the calling task. A message is encoded into
one buffer and sent with one CSocket::Send() call. A mutex serializes all
access to the connections, so Publish(), Subscribe() and Unsubscribe() can
be called from any task. The message handler is called with this mutex
held, so it may call Subscribe()/Unsubscribe(), but it should return
quickly.

The Circle TCP stack blocks the sender only, if more than 64 KByte are
queued for a connection already (i.e. a subscriber is too slow or stalled).
If such a subscriber does not accept data within the send timeout (default
1 second, see SetSendTimeout()), it is disconnected, instead of blocking the
publisher forever. libzmq would drop the messages for this subscriber
instead (high-water mark).

All ZeroMQ objects must be used on core 0 only, like the rest of the Circle
network stack (see doc/multicore.txt). On a multi-core system, you can hand
over data from other cores to a task on core 0 using a spin-lock protected
buffer (see sample/50-ethercatzmq for an example of this approach).

The limits are defined in zmqsocket.h (ZMQ_MAX_PEERS: 8 connections per
socket, ZMQ_MAX_ENDPOINTS: 4 Bind()/Connect() calls per socket), in
zmqmessage.h (ZMQ_MAX_FRAMES) and in zmtpconnection.h (ZMQ_MAX_FRAME_SIZE). A
received message with more than ZMQ_MAX_FRAMES frames is dropped, a peer,
which sends a frame larger than ZMQ_MAX_FRAME_SIZE, is disconnected.


SAMPLE

The sample in the subdirectory sample/ publishes the topics "circle.uptime"
(a two-part message) and "circle.counter" (a three-part message) every
second on port 5556 and subscribes the topic "pc." from a publisher on port
5557 of another computer. Received messages are written to the log.

Build it with:

	cd addon/zeromq/sample
	make

The computer running the remote publisher is set with this option in the
file cmdline.txt on the SD card (default 192.168.0.10):

	zmqpub=192.168.0.10 zmqport=5557

On the computer run these Python scripts (requires "pip install pyzmq"):

	python3 sample/subscriber.py <ip-of-raspberry-pi>	# shows the published messages
	python3 sample/publisher.py				# sends "pc.hello" to Circle

The sample uses DHCP by default. See the beginning of kernel.cpp to use a
static IP address.
